--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Copyleft in the install graph (not redistributed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two dependency families in the resolved tree carry copyleft terms. Neither is
bundled into the published tarball, and neither is modified by this project.

  @img/sharp-<platform>  Apache-2.0 / Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later
    Reached through `sharp@0.35.5`, a hard dependency of
    `@huggingface/transformers@4.3.2` (Konoha's ONNX embedding backend), which
    requires `sharp` at module load (`dist/transformers.node.cjs`). Each package
    carries a prebuilt libvips binary for one platform, which `sharp` loads for
    image decoding; Konoha calls transformers for text embeddings and never
    processes an image. The declared license is not uniform across the builds:
    the Windows packages state `Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later`, the Linux and
    macOS packages state `Apache-2.0` for the same libvips payload. Treated here
    as copyleft on every host, which is why the dependency gate allowlists the
    whole `@img/sharp-` prefix rather than the expression it happens to see.
    Each package is platform-specific and marked `optional: true` in
    `pnpm-lock.yaml`, so exactly one is installed per host, from its own
    published tarball, into the consumer's install tree.

    Konoha does not redistribute this package: it is not in `bundleDependencies`
    and not in the published tarball. It is also not a removable dependency —
    `sharp` is required unconditionally by the transformers Node entry, so
    dropping it (for example with `--omit=optional`) removes the embedding
    backend entirely.

  lightningcss, lightningcss-<platform>  MPL-2.0
    Transitive build tooling of `vite` / `@tailwindcss/vite`, both devDependencies
    of `apps/web`. Executed only while building the frontend. MPL-2.0 is
    file-level copyleft: it covers changes to lightningcss's own files, of which
    there are none here, and does not extend to the CSS or JS it compiles. The
    built frontend `apps/web/build/` does ship in the tarball, but it contains
    compiled assets with no lightningcss code — `grep -r lightningcss
    apps/web/build` finds nothing.

